 "path": "memory/continual-arc/rotationally-masked-grid.md",
 "content": "# Continual-ARC: masked rectangular patch via 180° rotation

For this task family, a rectangular all-zero region is a masked patch. Reconstruct it by copying the same-sized patch from the diametrically opposite location in the 24×24 grid, rotated 180° (reverse both row and column order). For a hole occupying inclusive 0-based rows `r0..r1` and columns `c0..c1` in an `N×N` grid, output cell `(i,j)` is input cell `(N-1-(r0+i), N-1-(c0+j))`. Equivalently, take the opposite patch and rotate it 180°.

Demonstration evidence: one 8×5 hole yielded the opposite patch rotated 180°; a 4×3 hole likewise matched; the solved 3×3 test hole at rows 9–11, cols 14–16 yielded `[[6,8,4],[5,9,4],[8,9,4]]` by this rule. Locate the full zero rectangle first; output dimensions equal the hole dimensions. Verify there is a corresponding complete nonzero patch opposite before submitting."
}
